  "body": "## What changed\r\n\r\nThe cause of #1993 is in the camera, not in the envelope. The envelope already is a cylinder about world Z for a lathe (`crates/post/src/machine/envelope.rs:27-60`). The turning paths also use world Z as the spindle axis. But `Camera::basis_right` (`crates/ui/src/viewport.rs`, before this PR: `normalize([fy, -fx, 0.0])`) always crossed the view with world +Z. Thus world Z could never lie across the screen, and each cylinder about Z stood vertical.\r\n\r\n- New `crates/ui/src/view_frame.rs`: `ViewFrame { Mill, Lathe }`, a proper rotation between the orbit frame and the world. `Mill` is the identity. `Lathe`: local x = world -Z, local y = world +Y, local z = world +X.\r\n- `Camera` gets a `frame` field. `eye` and `basis_right` map through the frame. `basis_up`, `mvp`, `ray_through`, `dolly_cursor`, the traced-stock eye and the view cube read that one basis. Draw and pick stay on one matrix.\r\n- `Camera::toward` converts the world direction of the view cube into the frame. `Camera::pan` now uses the Z part of the right vector (it was always zero before).\r\n- `Camera::with_frame` sets a frame and its home view. `view_frame::sync` runs at the top of `viewport_panel` in each frame. It reads `camcraft_app::machine::kind_of` (the machine of the active setup). A change of frame takes the home view of the frame. Fit and \"nothing to frame\" keep the frame.\r\n- Screen rule for a lathe: the right-handed program frame (ISO 841) does not change. The home view puts world +Z (the spindle axis, away from the chuck) to the right and world +X (the cross-slide) up. World +Y then points to the viewer. The frame comes from the machine kind in the machine data, not from a hard-coded hand per machine.\r\n\r\nDisplay only. The world, the WCS, the toolpaths and the posted G-code do not change: no post or generator code reads `Camera`. The frame change is view state, not a command (the test checks that the history does not change).\r\n\r\nThe decision record is `docs/decisions/1993-lathe-envelope-axis.md`.\r\n\r\n## What #2019 still needs\r\n\r\nThis PR does the camera part. #2019 stays open for:\r\n\r\n- The world gizmo: show Z and X only on a lathe, and Y only when the machine has a Y axis.\r\n- A decision for mill-turn machines (this PR keeps them in the milling frame).\r\n- View cube face names for the lathe frame, if the maintainer wants them (the cube now names world directions in each frame).\r\n- The pin that the posted program of a turning golden does not change.\r\n\r\n## Overlap\r\n\r\nPR #2006 changes `crates/ui/src/work_envelope.rs`. This PR does not touch that file. PR #1689 changes `Camera::eye` and `Camera::mvp` in `crates/ui/src/viewport.rs`. This PR changes `Camera::eye` too, so a small merge conflict can occur in `eye`.\r\n\r\n## Refresh (merge of main 17a8c6e76)\r\n\r\n- New head: 4eb577be305761c352696384cf4426ae8f207de4 (old head c620c1255b710ce1bce0e368c542c94db255f901).\r\n- `crates/ui/src/egui_app/tests.rs`: both sides added a test module after `mod work_envelope;`. The merge keeps the two: `mod lathe_view;` (#1993) and `mod camera_target;` (#1997).\r\n- `crates/ui/src/state.rs` merged without a conflict. `reset_for_open` now calls `frame_all` (#1997). `frame_all` keeps the camera frame (this PR), and `view_frame::sync` keeps the target and the distance. Thus an open frames the part, and a lathe keeps its frame.\r\n- Tests after the merge (debug, `cargo test -p camcraft-ui --lib`, filters lathe_view, camera_target, view_frame, work_envelope, camera, frame, viewcube, orbit, pan): 63 passed, 0 failed, 1 ignored. This includes `lathe_view::*` (3 tests), `camera_target::frame_target_is_the_same_with_the_envelope_on_and_off` and `camera_target::open_frames_the_part_with_the_envelope_on_and_off`.\r\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-ui --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean.\r\n- Delta class: DELTA (one hand-resolved file, `crates/ui/src/egui_app/tests.rs`).\r\n\r\n## Tests\r\n\r\nPinned by these tests in `crates/ui/src/egui_app/tests/lathe_view.rs`:\r\n\r\n- `a_lathe_lays_the_envelope_along_the_spindle_axis_left_to_right`: through the real shell, a lathe machine file gives the lathe frame and its home view. Screen right is world +Z and screen up is world +X. The envelope ends stay at the Z travel (-197, 3). The projected spindle axis is level, +Z is to the right, and the X radius goes up. The history does not change.\r\n- `a_mill_again_takes_the_milling_view_back`: a mill machine file again gives the milling frame and the house isometric.\r\n- `the_lathe_frame_keeps_pick_pan_and_the_view_cube_on_one_basis`: in the lathe frame, the basis is orthonormal and right-handed, the centre ray hits the target, a pan moves along the spindle axis, and the view cube and orbit keep the frame.\r\n\r\nExisting tests run (debug, `cargo test -p camcraft-ui --lib`, filters law_tests, viewcube, eye_geom, view_names, orbit, pan, iso, box_select, one_pick_rule, wcs_circle, wcs_part_point, wcs_pick_click, work_envelope, frame, edge_click, review_1710, camera, lathe_view): 144 passed, 0 failed. `cargo clippy -p camcraft-ui --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean.\r\n\r\n## Not exercised\r\n\r\n- No visible app window was opened. The screenshots below come from an offscreen render.\r\n- The pixel snapshots (release lane) are not run. The lathe view does not change a mill snapshot.\r\n\r\nChangelog: The viewport shows a lathe with its spindle axis from left to right and the cross-slide up, so the work envelope lies along the spindle axis (#1993).\r\n\r\nEffort: high (no escalation)\r\n\r\nFixes #1993\r\n\r\n## Screenshots\r\n\r\nMethod: the real shell and the viewport, rendered offscreen at 1280x800 by the UI test harness. No app window was opened. The same steps run before and after: mount a lathe machine with Z travel -197 to 3, turn on \"Show work envelope\", then set the camera target to (0, 0, -97) and the distance to 420.\r\n\r\nBefore (origin/main, 2f6de7c5b). The envelope cylinder stands upright:\r\n\r\n![before: lathe envelope](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/NeoTech/Camcraft/1f3a51015cc13780c217388ed587a932e61ee5ea/pr-2023/lathe-before.png)\r\n\r\nAfter (this PR, c620c1255). The spindle axis runs left to right. The cylinder lies along it:\r\n\r\n![after: lathe envelope](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/NeoTech/Camcraft/1f3a51015cc13780c217388ed587a932e61ee5ea/pr-2023/lathe-after.png)\r\n\r\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
